Design Notes: What Flare Is — and Isn’t (Yet)

Flare is deliberately narrow.

It does not attempt to solve all alignment problems, and it does not claim to “understand” human psychology. It addresses a specific, under-served failure mode in current AI systems: relational drift under recursion.

Early feedback has reinforced three things:

This failure mode is real.
RLHF and content moderation do not reliably prevent identity fusion, rescuer dynamics, or reassurance spirals — particularly when systems loop.

A middleware boundary layer is the right place to intervene.
It is auditable, tunable by context, and does not require retraining foundation models.

The hard problems are now implementation problems, not conceptual ones.

We are explicit about current limitations:

Surface-text detection will always have false positives and false negatives.

Cultural norms around relational language vary and require recalibration, not one-size-fits-all rules.

Distinguishing productive recursion from compulsive looping is a non-trivial design challenge.

These are not reasons to avoid boundary enforcement.
They are reasons to design it carefully.

In high-risk contexts — especially education and child-facing systems — the cost of mild over-constraint is lower than the cost of unchecked enmeshment. Flare is designed to be configurable precisely because no single relational stance is safe everywhere.

Flare is not a complete solution.
It is a missing layer.
